				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">head title hook</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					<code>.cm-head h1</code> already sizes the title, so
					<code>.cm-head__title</code> is the hook for a title
					that is not an <code>h1</code> &mdash; an
					<code>aria-label</code> on a landmark, or a
					<code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> leading a section. It reads
					the same size either way, which is the point: the
					selector is a pair, not two competing rules.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-head" style="padding:0">
					<p class="cm-head__title">a title that is not an h1</p>
				</div>
			</section>
			<!-- ==================================================== selection
			     A data surface needs two marks the library did not own: a
			     SELECTED row and a live/off indicator. Twelve consumers
			     each invented one, in hue - 28 distinct raw colours across
			     one app. Both are weight and shape here, so they survive a
			     greyscale print and a colour-blind read. -->
			<section id="selection" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="selection + state marks">
A selected row takes a left rule and a faint tint rather than an outline: an outline competes with the row's own border, and the weight belongs on one edge so a column of them scans as a column.
				</SectionHead>
				<div style="display:grid;gap:var(--space-3);max-width:34rem">
					<div class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
						<div class="cm-row__body">
							<span class="cm-dot cm-dot--on" aria-hidden="true"></span>
							<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
						</div>
						<p class="cm-head__sub">live &mdash; filled dot</p>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
						<div class="cm-row__body">
							<span class="cm-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
							<span class="cm-row__title">old.example.com</span>
						</div>
						<p class="cm-head__sub">disabled &mdash; hollow dot</p>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-section cm-section--pad cm-section--on">
						<div class="cm-row__body">
							<span class="cm-dot cm-dot--on" aria-hidden="true"></span>
							<span class="cm-row__title">selected.example.com</span>
						</div>
						<p class="cm-head__sub">selected &mdash; left rule + tint</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">check row</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					The box is drawn by the base layer, so the control needs no
					class. What needs a class is the <strong>row</strong>: it carries
					the tap floor, so the word and the box are one target.
				</p>
				<div style="display:flex;gap:var(--space-4);flex-wrap:wrap">
					<label class="cm-check"><input type="checkbox" checked /> preserve path</label>
					<label class="cm-check"><input type="checkbox" /> force SSL</label>
					<label class="cm-check"><input type="checkbox" /> HSTS preload</label>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">action toolbar</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					The bar that acts on a selection sticks to the
					<strong>bottom</strong>: the count lives at the top of the page
					and the actions belong where the thumb already is.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-toolbar">
					<span class="cm-toolbar__count" aria-live="polite">3 items selected</span>
					<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm">enable all</button>
					<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm">disable all</button>
					<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--primary">delete all</button>
					<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--ghost">clear</button>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">inline edit</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					A data surface is mostly values you want to change in place.
					The display half is a <strong>dotted underline</strong>, not a
					box: a box on every cell turns a table into a form.
				</p>
				<div style="display:flex;gap:var(--space-5);align-items:baseline;flex-wrap:wrap">
					<span class="cm-inline" tabindex="0">app.example.com</span>
					<span class="cm-inline cm-inline--wide" tabindex="0">a-very-long-forward-hostname.example.com</span>
					<input class="cm-inline__input" style="max-width:14rem" value="editing" aria-label="editing state">
				</div>


			<!-- ==================================================== code
			     The one surface the runtime had and the library never
			     showed. [data-cm-copy] has been bound since it shipped
			     and every consumer has it vendored, but not one page
			     had a button using it, so nobody could reach the
			     capability and nobody could see a failure in it. Two
			     blocks here, on purpose: the bug that made every copy
			     button on a page copy the FIRST block only, and the
			     double-bind, both need a second button to exist. -->
			<section id="stack" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="record stack">
A vertical run of records. The stack is opt-in rather than baked into the list, because a ledger of rows can be flush (each row's own border does the separating) or stacked (cards with air between them), and the list element cannot know which one it is.
				</SectionHead>
				<div style="display:grid;gap:var(--space-5);grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(15rem,1fr))">
					<div>
						<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">stacked</p>
						<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack">
							<li class="cm-row cm-section cm-section--pad">
								<span class="cm-row__body">
									<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
									<span class="cm-row__meta">/health</span>
								</span>
							</li>
							<li class="cm-row cm-section cm-section--pad">
								<span class="cm-row__body">
									<span class="cm-row__title">cdn.example.com</span>
									<span class="cm-row__meta">/assets</span>
								</span>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div>
						<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">flush</p>
						<ul class="cm-rows">
							<li class="cm-row">
								<span class="cm-row__body">
									<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
									<span class="cm-row__meta">/health</span>
								</span>
							</li>
							</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div>
					<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">tight, the ledger case</p>
					<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack cm-stack--tight">
						<li class="cm-row">
							<span class="cm-row__body">app.example.com</span>
							<span class="cm-row__meta">/health</span>
						</li>
						<li class="cm-row">
							<span class="cm-row__body">cdn.example.com</span>
							<span class="cm-row__meta">/assets</span>
						</li>
						<li class="cm-row">
							<span class="cm-row__body">api.example.com</span>
							<span class="cm-row__meta">/v2</span>
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
						A run of BORDERED panels is a page of regions and gets the
						default gap; a run of hairline-separated records is one table
						and stays tight. The gap between siblings has to beat the
						padding inside a sibling or the grouping isn't legible.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

					<section id="density" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
						<SectionHead title="density and rhythm helpers">
							These exist because the rhythm was being written by hand in page
							markup: six call sites each passed `gap: var(--space-N)` inline.
							A vertical rhythm that lives in markup cannot be reviewed, cannot
							be shown here, and is invisible to every guard that reads the
							library. The app now names a step and the library owns the value.
						</SectionHead>
						<div class="cm-stack">
							<div>
								<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">the four stack steps, tight to loose</p>
								<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack cm-stack--tight">
									<li class="cm-row"><span class="cm-row__body">--tight</span></li>
								</ul>
								<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack cm-stack--snug" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<li class="cm-row"><span class="cm-row__body">--snug</span></li>
								</ul>
								<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack cm-stack--airy" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<li class="cm-row"><span class="cm-row__body">--airy</span></li>
								</ul>
								<ul class="cm-rows cm-stack cm-stack--loose" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<li class="cm-row"><span class="cm-row__body">--loose</span></li>
								</ul>
							</div>
							<div>
								<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">a row that wraps, and a control group that stacks</p>
								<div class="cm-card__body cm-row cm-row--wrap">
									<span class="cm-chip">one</span>
									<span class="cm-chip">two</span>
									<span class="cm-chip">three</span>
									<span class="cm-chip">four</span>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-btn-group cm-btn-group--stack cm-push" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--primary">Save</button>
									<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--ghost">Cancel</button>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-btn-group cm-btn-group--split" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--ghost">Back up</button>
									<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--primary">Restore</button>
								</div>
							</div>
							<div>
								<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">a bounded code block, a taller field, a grid seg, a hint</p>
								<pre class="cm-code cm-code--scroll">{'{\n  "listen": "443",\n  "target": "10.0.0.8:8443"\n}'}</pre>
								<div class="cm-field" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<label class="cm-field__label">metadata</label>
									<textarea class="cm-field__control cm-field--textarea">{'{\n  "owner": "infra"\n}'}</textarea>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-seg cm-seg--grid" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									<button class="cm-seg__opt is-active">On</button>
									<button class="cm-seg__opt">Off</button>
								</div>
								<p class="cm-head__sub cm-hint" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
									a hint wraps to the next line and sits at reduced contrast
								</p>
							</div>
							<div>
								<p class="cm-row__meta" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">a tight card head, a spaced kicker, a padded block, a filling child</p>
								<div class="cm-card">
									<div class="cm-card__head cm-card__head--tight cm-row">
										<span class="cm-row__title">tight head</span>
									</div>
									<div class="cm-card__body">
										<div class="cm-head-row">
											<div class="cm-fill">a child that takes the slack but may still shrink</div>
											<span class="cm-push">trailing</span>
										</div>
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--spaced">spaced kicker</div>
								<div class="cm-spinner-row cm-header-block">
									<span>padded block</span>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>
					</section>
						<section id="badge" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="page head">
The title row every page starts with: a square glyph, the name, one line of what the page is for, and the page's own action. The badge is an inset square rather than the seven gradients it replaces, because a hue is the only signal that fails in greyscale.
							</SectionHead>
							<div class="cm-head-row">
								<span class="cm-head__badge" aria-hidden="true">
									<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="m4.9 4.9 14.2 14.2"/></svg>
								</span>
								<div class="cm-head-row__text">
									<div class="cm-head__title">Dead Hosts</div>
									<div class="cm-head__sub">404 catch-all domains</div>
								</div>
								<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--primary cm-head-row__action">Add Dead Host</button>
							</div>
						</section>
												<section id="tblaction" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="table action">
A row's control, in the table's own visual language. A <code>&lt;tr onClick&gt;</code> is not a control - it is not focusable and Enter does nothing - so this is a real button, drawn as text so the grid does not become a stack of chrome. The tap floor is its own padding box. On a coarse pointer the arrow is always visible, because there is no hover to reveal it.
							</SectionHead>
							<div class="cm-table-wrap">
								<table class="cm-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Host</th>
											<th class="cm-table__num">Total</th>
											<th class="cm-table__num">2xx</th>
											<th class="cm-table__num">5xx</th>
											<th class="cm-table__num">Avg RT</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										{[
											['plex.mrx.sh', '4.2k', '4.1k', '9', '12.4ms'],
											['app.example.com', '1.9k', '1.6k', '210', '88.0ms'],
											['static.example.com', '640', '640', '0', '3.1ms'],
										].map(([host, total, two, five, rt]) => (
											<tr>
												<td>
													<button type="button" class="cm-table__action">
														{host}
														<svg
															viewBox="0 0 24 24"
															fill="none"
															stroke="currentColor"
															stroke-width="2"
															aria-hidden="true"
															>
															<path d="M7 17 17 7M8 7h9v9" />
														</svg>
													</button>
												</td>
												<td class="cm-table__num">{total}</td>
												<td class="cm-table__num">{two}</td>
												<td class="cm-table__num">{five}</td>
												<td class="cm-table__num">{rt}</td>
											</tr>
										))}
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
							<div class="cm-cols cm-cols--2" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">
								<div class="cm-block">
									<h3 class="cm-head__sub">Top paths</h3>
									<div class="cm-meters">
										{[
											['/api/health', 100, '4.2k'],
											['/assets/app.js', 44, '1.9k'],
											['/library/sections', 15, '640'],
										].map(([label, pct, value]) => (
											<div class="cm-meter cm-meter--row">
												<div class="cm-meter__track" aria-hidden="true">
													<span class="cm-meter__fill" style={`--cm-meter-fill: ${pct}%`} />
												</div>
												<span class="cm-meter__label">{label}</span>
												<span class="cm-meter__val">{value}</span>
												<span class="cm-meter__note">{(pct / 4).toFixed(1)}%</span>
											</div>
										))}
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-block">
									<h3 class="cm-head__sub">Referrers</h3>
									<div class="cm-meters">
										{[
											['app.example.com', 100, '2.1k'],
											['(direct)', 62, '1.3k'],
											['news.ycombinator.com', 11, '221'],
										].map(([label, pct, value]) => (
											<div class="cm-meter cm-meter--row">
												<div class="cm-meter__track" aria-hidden="true">
													<span class="cm-meter__fill" style={`--cm-meter-fill: ${pct}%`} />
												</div>
												<span class="cm-meter__label">{label}</span>
												<span class="cm-meter__val">{value}</span>
												<span class="cm-meter__note">{(pct / 4).toFixed(1)}%</span>
											</div>
										))}
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>
						</section>
						<section id="table" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="table">
For records a person scans <em>across</em> rather than one at a time - a log is the case that cannot be cards, because the columns have to line up. The header is sticky, because row 400 otherwise has no idea which column it is in. Status codes carry a weight and a marker, not a hue: a wall of red and green is unreadable in greyscale and to anyone with colour vision. Five of the seven headers are sort controls. Each is a &#60;button&#62; inside a &#60;th scope="col" aria-sort&#62;, which is the shape a sortable column actually has: the sort state lives on the th, where the accessibility tree reads it, and the dot is drawn by CSS from that same attribute so the visible mark and the announced state cannot drift apart. A &#123;&lt;th onClick&#125;&#125; is not a control - it cannot be focused and Enter does nothing to it.
							</SectionHead>
							<div class="cm-table-wrap">
								<table class="cm-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol" aria-sort="none">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Time</button>
										</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol" aria-sort="descending">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Method</button>
										</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol" aria-sort="none">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Host</button>
										</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol" aria-sort="none">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Path</button>
										</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol" aria-sort="none">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Status</button>
										</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__num">Duration</th>
										<th scope="col" class="cm-table__sortcol right" aria-sort="ascending">
											<button type="button" class="cm-table__sort">Client</button>
										</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										{LOG.map(r => (
											<tr>
												<td>{r.t}</td>
												<td>{r.m}</td>
												<td class="cm-table__truncate">{r.host}</td>
												<td class="cm-table__truncate">{r.path}</td>
												<td><span class={`cm-status-code cm-status-code--${r.k}`}>{r.g} {r.s}</span></td>
												<td class="cm-table__num">{r.d}</td>
												<td>{r.ip}</td>
											</tr>
										))}
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</section>
						<section id="json" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="code: json">
The monospace surface for structured text, in the two forms it is always used: a block you read and a block you edit. Same type, same frame, so switching between them does not move the text. Not to be confused with <code>cm-code-input</code>, which is the centred one-time-code field.
							</SectionHead>
							<div class="cm-row" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-sm)">
								<details class="cm-disclosure cm-disclosure--flush" open>
									<summary class="cm-disclosure__summary">
										<span class="cm-disclosure__mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span>
										<span class="cm-disclosure__title">meta</span>
										<span class="cm-row__meta">(42 chars)</span>
									</summary>
									<div class="cm-disclosure__body cm-stack">
										<pre class="cm-code">&#123;
  "forward_port": 8096
&#125;</pre>
										<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--ghost" style="align-self:flex-start">Edit</button>
									</div>
								</details>
							</div>
						</section>
						<section id="rowon" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="selected row">
<code>cm-row--on</code> is the list-row form of the selected treatment, and <code>button.cm-row</code> is a row that is itself the control. Both mark state with an inset rule rather than a tint: a coloured row is the one signal that disappears in greyscale print, and a selected row is information the reader has to see.
							</SectionHead>
							<ul class="cm-rows" style="max-width:34rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-sm)">
								<li>
									<button type="button" class="cm-row cm-row--on">
										<span class="cm-row__body">
											<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
											<span class="cm-row__desc">proxy · :443</span>
										</span>
										<span class="cm-chip cm-chip--ok">live</span>
									</button>
								</li>
								<li>
									<button type="button" class="cm-row">
										<span class="cm-row__body">
											<span class="cm-row__title">old.example.com</span>
											<span class="cm-row__desc">redirect → :80</span>
										</span>
										<span class="cm-chip cm-chip--warn">draining</span>
									</button>
								</li>
								<li>
									<button type="button" class="cm-row">
										<span class="cm-row__body">
											<span class="cm-row__title">off.example.com</span>
											<span class="cm-row__desc">no upstream</span>
										</span>
									</button>
								</li>
							</ul>
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-5)">
								<code>cm-row__meta--wrap</code> is for a meta slot that carries
								a CONTROL rather than a label. The plain meta slot is nowrap and
								unshrinkable, which is right for a status and wrong for a
								&lt;select&gt;: the row body clips it, so the control is unreachable
								on a phone. On the modifier the slot wraps and shrinks, so the row
								still fits at 390px.
							</p>
							<ul class="cm-rows" style="max-width:34rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-sm)">
								<li>
									<div class="cm-row">
										<span class="cm-row__body cm-row__body--wrap">
											<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
											<span class="cm-row__meta cm-row__meta--wrap">
												<span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
												<label class="cm-sr-only" for="cm-demo-scheme">Forward scheme</label>
												<select id="cm-demo-scheme">
													<option value="http">http</option>
													<option value="https" selected>https</option>
												</select>
												<span aria-hidden="true">://</span>
												<span class="cm-inline">origin.internal:8443</span>
											</span>
										</span>
									</div>
								</li>
							</ul>
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-5)">
								<code>cm-inline__input--tight</code> sizes the INPUT rather than
								the display span. <code>cm-inline--wide</code> is a max-width on the
								span; an IP or a port is short, and
								<code>width: 100%</code> inside a meta strip sized it to
								whatever space was left - which on a phone is none, so the
								field collapsed and there was nothing to click.
							</p>
							<div class="cm-row" style="max-width:34rem;gap:var(--space-2);align-items:center">
								<span class="cm-row__meta">&rarr;</span>
								<input class="cm-inline__input cm-inline__input--tight" value="10.0.0.7" aria-label="Forward IP" />
								<span class="cm-row__meta">:</span>
								<input class="cm-inline__input cm-inline__input--tight" value="8443" aria-label="Forward port" />
						</div>

						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-5)">
						<code>cm-row__meta--nowrap</code> is the other direction from
						<code>--wrap</code>: a meta slot carrying ONE value that must not
						split, given its own line by <code>cm-row__body--wrap</code> rather
						than being starved of one. <code>cm-row__meta--tight</code> takes
						the gap back to zero and restores a single space at the first
						boundary, because the parts of <code>&rarr; host:port</code> are
						ONE value, and a flex gap puts a space on <em>both</em> sides of
						the colon. MEASURED in WebKit at 402 and 375: the tight row's colon
						sits 0px from the address and 0px from the port, and the only
						space in the value is the 0.35em in front of the address.
						</p>
						<ul class="cm-rows" style="max-width:34rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-sm)">
						<li>
						<div class="cm-row">
						<span class="cm-row__body cm-row__body--wrap">
						<span class="cm-row__title">app.example.com</span>
						<span class="cm-row__meta cm-row__meta--nowrap">
						<span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
						<span class="cm-inline">192.168.1.104:8443</span>
						</span>
						</span>
						</div>
						</li>
						<li>
						<div class="cm-row">
						<span class="cm-row__body cm-row__body--wrap">
						<span class="cm-row__title">api.example.com</span>
						<span class="cm-row__meta cm-row__meta--nowrap cm-row__meta--tight">
						<span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
						<span>192.168.1.104</span>
						<span>:</span>
						<span>80</span>
						</span>
						</span>
						</div>
						</li>
						</ul>
						</section>
						<section id="switch" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="switch">
							For "this whole behaviour is on or off" - as distinct from <code>cm-check</code>, which is "include this row". The knob's position carries the state, not its colour. The <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> is the control, transparent and on top: it is the tap target, the focus target and the form value, so a keyboard or screen reader reaches a real checkbox rather than a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a click handler.
								<span slot="action">
									<button type="button" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary">Add provider</button>
								</span>
							</SectionHead>
							<div class="cm-card" style="max-width:26rem">
								<div class="cm-card__body cm-stack">
									<label class="cm-check">
										<input type="checkbox" checked />
										HTTP/2 to the upstream
									</label>
									<div class="cm-row" style="justify-content:space-between">
										<div class="cm-row__title">
											WebSocket upgrade
											<div class="cm-row__meta">rewrite the Host header</div>
										</div>
										<label class="cm-switch">
											<input type="checkbox" checked />
											<span class="cm-switch__track">
												<span class="cm-switch__knob"></span>
											</span>
										</label>
									</div>
									<div class="cm-row" style="justify-content:space-between">
										<div class="cm-row__title">
											Force a trailing slash
											<div class="cm-row__meta">off by default</div>
										</div>
										<label class="cm-switch">
											<input type="checkbox" />
											<span class="cm-switch__track">
												<span class="cm-switch__knob"></span>
											</span>
										</label>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>
						</section>
						<section id="sheet" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
							<SectionHead title="dialog, as a side sheet">
The same native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> as the confirm modal, anchored to the right edge instead of the centre. Only the frame changes, so it keeps the focus trap, Escape, the top layer and the inertness of the page behind it - all of which a hand-rolled <code>position: fixed</code> panel gives up. Full-width and flush below 640px.
							</SectionHead>
							<dialog class="cm-dialog cm-dialog--sheet" style="position:static;height:16rem;margin:0 auto;max-height:16rem">
								<div class="cm-dialog__head">
									<div>
										<div class="cm-dialog__title">app.example.com</div>
									</div>
									<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-btn--ghost">Close</button>
								</div>
								<div class="cm-dialog__body">
									<p>
										Rendered as a specimen: the frame is the only
										thing this modifier changes.
									</p>
								</div>
							</dialog>
						</section>
						<section id="choice" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="choice + disclosure">
A compact set of exclusive choices, and a summary row that reveals a panel. Both mark their selected / open state with an inset rule, because a thicker border would widen the box and walk the row sideways.
				</SectionHead>
				<div style="display:grid;gap:var(--space-5);max-width:34rem">
					<div class="cm-field">
						<span class="cm-field__label">Directive</span>
						<div class="cm-seg" role="group" aria-label="Directive">
							<button type="button" class="cm-seg__opt" aria-pressed="true">allow</button>
							<button type="button" class="cm-seg__opt" aria-pressed="false">deny</button>
						</div>
					</div>
					<details class="cm-disclosure">
						<summary class="cm-disclosure__summary">
							<span class="cm-disclosure__mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9656;</span>
							<span class="cm-disclosure__title">10.0.0.0/8</span>
							<span class="cm-chip cm-chip--ok">allow</span>
							<button
								class="cm-icon-btn cm-icon-btn--bare cm-disclosure__action"
								title="Delete"
								aria-label="Delete rule">&#215;</button>
						</summary>
						<div class="cm-disclosure__body">
							<p class="cm-row__meta">The body is hidden until the summary is activated.</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</section>
<section id="code" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="code">
A <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> is an element default, so it is on-brand before you add a class. <code>.cm-codebar</code> is the wrapper that gives it a label row and a home for a copy button, and <code>.cm-copy</code> is the button. Press one: the label changes, and the state class is the only feedback &mdash; this system has no chroma, so the change has to be legible in greyscale.
				</SectionHead>

				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-install"
					lang="bash"
					code={`curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.sh | sh`}
				/>

				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-config"
					lang="typescript"
					code={`export const site = {
  name: 'oem',
  theme: 'dark',
} satisfies SiteConfig;`}
				/>

				<!-- The guard. Shown because the wiring is the whole point:
				     a consumer that owns its own <head> must inline this
				     file, and the ?raw import is the only shape that keeps
				     it ahead of the stylesheets. -->
				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-guard"
					lang="astro"
					code={`---
// <head> runs before the runtime bundle exists, so it cannot ask the
// runtime for its keys. Inline the shipped file instead of hand-rolling
// one, or a theme saved under a legacy key is missed and every returning
// light-theme visitor gets a black flash.
import themeGuard from '../js/cli-mono-theme-guard.js?raw';
---
<head>
  <!-- before <meta charset>, before every stylesheet -->
  <script is:inline set:html={themeGuard} />
</head>`}
				/>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">states</h3>
				<div class="cm-spec">
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>idle</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample">
							<span class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-copy">
								<span class="cm-copy__state" aria-hidden="true"></span>
								<span>copy</span>
							</span>
						</span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>default</code></span>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>copied</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample">
							<span class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-copy is-copied">
								<span class="cm-copy__state" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
								<span>copied</span>
							</span>
						</span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>runtime</code></span>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>error</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample">
							<span class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm cm-copy is-error">
								<span class="cm-copy__state" aria-hidden="true">&#10007;</span>
								<span>press &#8984;c</span>
							</span>
						</span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>runtime</code></span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- ==================================================== overlays
			     Five components that sit ON TOP of the page, and three of
			     them are the platform's element: <dialog>, [popover] and
			     hover/focus. Nothing here has a runtime that reimplements
			     what a browser already does correctly. -->
			<section id="overlays" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="overlays">
Five surfaces that sit on top of the page. Four of the five are native elements doing native things &mdash; a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, a <code>[popover]</code>, a hover, a <code>hidden</code> attribute. Only the tablist needs script, because the platform ships no tab widget and the roving tabindex is the one part of the ARIA contract CSS cannot express.
				</SectionHead>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">tabs</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					Tab with the keyboard to leave the row, then use the arrows: one tab
					is in the tab order at a time, which is the WAI-ARIA pattern. The
					active tab is an <em>inset</em> rule rather than a thicker border,
					because 2px of border would widen every box and walk the row sideways.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-tabs">
					<div class="cm-tabs__list" role="tablist" aria-label="layer ownership">
						<button
							type="button"
							class="cm-tabs__tab"
							role="tab"
							id="tab-tokens"
							aria-selected="true"
							aria-controls="panel-tokens"
						>tokens</button>
						<button
							type="button"
							class="cm-tabs__tab"
							role="tab"
							id="tab-base"
							aria-selected="false"
							aria-controls="panel-base"
						>base</button>
						<button
							type="button"
							class="cm-tabs__tab"
							role="tab"
							id="tab-components"
							aria-selected="false"
							aria-controls="panel-components"
						>components</button>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-tokens" aria-labelledby="tab-tokens" tabindex="0">
						Every value, both themes. A hardcoded hex in the layer below is a
						rebrand bug, so this file is the contract.
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-base" aria-labelledby="tab-base" tabindex="0" hidden>
						Element defaults, prose rhythm, scrollbar, a11y helpers. A bare
						<code>&lt;input&gt;</code> is on-brand because it lives here, not
						behind a class.
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-components" aria-labelledby="tab-components" tabindex="0" hidden>
						The <code>.cm-*</code> classes: the arrangement <em>around</em> an
						element, which is the part that is actually a design decision.
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">dialog</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					A native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> opened with
					<code>showModal()</code>, so focus trapping, Escape, the top layer and
					the inertness of the page behind it are the browser's job, not ours.
					<code>::backdrop</code> paints <code>--scrim</code>, which is a token and
					differs per theme &mdash; the same alpha over near-black and near-white
					does not hide the page by the same amount.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-btn-group">
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn" data-cm-open="dlg-demo">open dialog</button>
				</div>
				<dialog class="cm-dialog" id="dlg-demo" aria-labelledby="dlg-demo-title">
					<form method="dialog" class="cm-dialog__head">
						<h3 class="cm-dialog__title" id="dlg-demo-title">Close the build?</h3>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-toast__close" aria-label="Close" value="close">&times;</button>
					</form>
					<div class="cm-dialog__body">
						<p>
							This is the native element. Tab is trapped here, Escape closes, and
							the page behind it is inert without a single line of script.
						</p>
						<p>
							The scrim is <code>--scrim</code>, and the two themes set it to
							different values for exactly that reason.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-dialog__foot">
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn" value="cancel">cancel</button>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary" value="confirm">close it</button>
					</div>
				</dialog>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">toast</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					A transient <code>.cm-alert</code>: the same border weights, the same
					glyphs, the same words. A second, prettier alert component is how one
					system quietly becomes two. Each retires itself after six seconds, or
					immediately via its close control.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-btn-group">
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn" data-cm-toast-demo="ok">toast ok</button>
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn" data-cm-toast-demo="warn">toast warn</button>
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn" data-cm-toast-demo="err">toast err</button>
				</div>
				<div class="cm-toast-region" data-cm-toasts role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">dropdown</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					The native Popover API. The trigger carries
					<code>popovertarget</code> and the panel carries <code>popover</code>,
					so light-dismiss, Escape and focus return are the browser's &mdash;
					and there is no runtime code for this component at all.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-dropdown">
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn" popovertarget="menu-demo" aria-haspopup="menu">
						actions <span aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span>
					</button>
					<div class="cm-dropdown__menu" id="menu-demo" popover role="menu" aria-label="row actions">
						<button type="button" class="cm-dropdown__item" role="menuitem">rename</button>
						<button type="button" class="cm-dropdown__item" role="menuitem">duplicate</button>
						<hr class="cm-dropdown__sep" />
						<button type="button" class="cm-dropdown__item" role="menuitem" aria-disabled="true">delete</button>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">tooltip</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					A real element rather than generated content, because
					<code>aria-describedby</code> has to resolve to something in the
					accessibility tree and a <code>::after</code> is not there. The trigger
					is focusable, so <code>:focus-within</code> reveals the tip from the
					keyboard with no JS. Hidden tips are
					<code>visibility: hidden</code>, not merely transparent: a
					transparent tip is still announced and still swallows a click.
				</p>
				<p class="cm-btn-group">
					<span class="cm-tooltip cm-tooltip--start">
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn" aria-describedby="tip-demo">
							--start
						</button>
						<span class="cm-tooltip__tip" id="tip-demo" role="tooltip">
							Pinned to the trigger's leading edge.
						</span>
					</span>
					<span class="cm-tooltip">
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn" aria-describedby="tip-demo-centre">
							centred
						</button>
						<span class="cm-tooltip__tip" id="tip-demo-centre" role="tooltip">
							Centred: the tip may be wider than the button.
						</span>
					</span>
					<span class="cm-tooltip cm-tooltip--end">
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn" aria-describedby="tip-demo-end">
							--end
						</button>
						<span class="cm-tooltip__tip" id="tip-demo-end" role="tooltip">
							Pinned to the trailing edge.
						</span>
					</span>
					<span class="cm-tooltip cm-tooltip--below cm-tooltip--start">
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn" aria-describedby="tip-demo-below">
							--below
						</button>
						<span class="cm-tooltip__tip" id="tip-demo-below" role="tooltip">
							Below, when there is no room above.
						</span>
					</span>
				</p>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					A tip is capped at the viewport, and the page root clips
					horizontal overflow, so an overlay that is wider than its
					trigger can never make the page scroll sideways. Prefer
					<code>--start</code> or <code>--end</code> near an edge: a
					centred tip is only fully visible when the trigger sits
					clear of both margins.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- ==================================================== records
			     A metric tile is not a key/value row and a work history
			     is not a link list, so both needed a real component. -->
			<section id="records" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="Records">
Stat tiles and a dated timeline. The tile count is data, so <code>.cm-stats</code> sizes its columns with <code>auto-fit</code> rather than a fixed number, and the timeline rail is drawn on the <em>item</em> so it stops at the last record on its own, with no script. (<code>auto-fit</code> fits the count to the width; it cannot balance a ragged last row — see the balanced band below for that.)
				</SectionHead>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">stat tiles</h3>
				<ul class="cm-stats">
					<Stat value="8+" label="years in IT" note="since 2014" />
					<Stat value="5,000+" label="users and devices" note="global estate" />
					<Stat value="3" label="CompTIA certs" note="A+, Tech+, ITF+" />
				</ul>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					The note line is optional. It is the qualifier that makes a
					number honest: “5,000+” on its own is a boast, “5,000+ across
					the estate” is a fact.
				</p>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6);margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					balanced bands, and a header row with a trailing chip
				</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">
					<code>auto-fit</code> cannot stop a stranded tile. It always fits as
					many columns as the width allows, so the last row keeps whatever
					is left — measured on a seven-tile band at 1280px: six columns
					of 158px and one tile alone beside 850px of nothing. A balanced
					band takes an explicit count, and the page that knows how many
					tiles it has is the page that sets it.
				</p>

				<ul class="cm-stats cm-stats--balance" style="--cm-stat-cols: 4">
					<Stat value="1" label="proxy host" />
					<Stat value="1" label="redirection" />
					<Stat value="3" label="streams" />
					<Stat value="1" label="dead host" />
					<Stat value="9" label="access lists" />
					<Stat value="24" label="certificates" />
					<Stat value="1" label="user" />
				</ul>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					Seven tiles over four columns: 4 + 3, one dead column, which is
					the best a single count can do for an odd total.
				</p>

				<div class="cm-head-row" style="margin-top:var(--space-7);margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					<div class="cm-head-row__text">
						<span class="cm-head__badge" aria-hidden="true">●</span>
						<h3 class="cm-section__title">A header row with a trailing chip</h3>
					</div>
					<span class="cm-tag cm-head-row__meta">24 total</span>
				</div>
				<div class="cm-cards cm-cards--balance">
					<div class="cm-tile">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Healthy</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">24</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__note">&gt;30 days</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tile">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Expiring soon</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">0</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__note">&lt;30 days</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tile">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Expired</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">0</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					The badge and the title share one line, and the chip is held
					against the right edge by <code>.cm-head-row__meta</code>. Three
					tiles over three columns: <code>.cm-cards--balance</code> is the
					same fix for a card row, where a fitted grid left two and one.
				</p>

				<div class="cm-row" style="margin-top:var(--space-5);align-items:stretch">
					<span class="cm-row__body">Trailing meta</span>
					<span class="cm-row__meta cm-row__meta--wrap cm-stick-top">top-aligned, not stretched</span>
					<span class="cm-row__meta cm-row__meta--wrap cm-push">last, so .cm-push holds it right</span>
				</div>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					Both are position utilities, and <code>.cm-push</code> only means
					something as the LAST child: <code>margin-left: auto</code>
					distributes the slack that follows it, so an element with
					siblings after it is pushed away from them instead of the edge.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-btn-group cm-push-group" style="margin-top:var(--space-4)">
					<span class="cm-tag">left</span>
					<span class="cm-tag cm-push">right</span>
				</div>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					<code>.cm-push</code> and <code>.cm-stick-top</code> replace four
					inline <code>marginLeft: 'auto'</code> copies in rpm. An inline
					style cannot be overridden by a stylesheet and cannot respond to
					a breakpoint; a class can do both.
				</p>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6);margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">timeline</h3>
				<ul class="cm-timeline">
					<TimelineItem
						period="2014 – 2017"
						role="Help Desk Technical Support"
						org="Union University"
						meta="Jackson, TN"
						body={'Phone-based support for university services. Setup of campus computers, printers and peripherals.'}
					/>
					<TimelineItem
						period="Feb 2022 – present"
						role="L2 Tech Analyst"
						org="Richemont"
						meta="Coral Gables, FL"
						body={'Owns HQ office IT for a global luxury group: device lifecycle, access management and compliance reporting.'}
						now
					/>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- ==================================================== cards
			     One subject that needs more room than a row, and a
			     number that is a proportion rather than a count. -->
			<section id="cards" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="Cards and meters">
A card is a unit of content with a title, so <code>.cm-cards</code> is a grid and <code>.cm-card</code> fills its cell — a two-line card and a six-line card in the same row still share a bottom edge. A meter is a number that happens to be drawn: the bar is <code>aria-hidden</code> and the value is real text, so the chart and the sentence can never disagree.
				</SectionHead>

				<ul class="cm-cards">
					<Card title="A+ 220-1101" sub="taken 2022 · lapsed 2025" accent>
						<p class="cm-card__text">
							Hardware and networking for a working technician. The
							domains below are the published exam weighting, not a
							score: this is what the exam actually asked.
						</p>
						<div class="cm-meters">
							<Meter label="Mobile devices" value="13%" pct={13} />
							<Meter label="Networking" value="23%" pct={23} variant="accent" />
							<Meter label="Hardware" value="25%" pct={25} />
							<Meter label="Virtualization and cloud" value="11%" pct={11} variant="faint" />
							<Meter label="Troubleshooting" value="28%" pct={28} />
						</div>

						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-5)">
							<code>--row</code> is the same meter on one line, for a
							ranked list. The track is the only element allowed to
							grow, so every bar starts at the same x and two
							lengths can be compared by their left edge - which
							is the only thing a bar chart is for. The value stays
							in a column you can scan down, with the share beside it in a
							column of its own, aligned on the decimal.
						</p>
						<div class="cm-meters">
							{[
								['/api/health', 100, '4.2k'],
								['/assets/app.js', 44, '1.9k'],
								['/library/sections', 15, '640'],
								['/old/path', 2, '92'],
							].map(([label, pct, value]) => (
								<div class="cm-meter cm-meter--row">
									<div class="cm-meter__track" aria-hidden="true">
										<span
											class="cm-meter__fill"
											style={`--cm-meter-fill: ${pct}%`}
										/>
									</div>
									<span class="cm-meter__label">{label}</span>
									<span class="cm-meter__val">{value}</span>
									<span class="cm-meter__note">{(pct / 4).toFixed(1)}%</span>
								</div>
							))}
						</div>
						<Fragment slot="foot">
							<a class="cm-card__link" href="https://example.com/a-plus">exam objectives →</a>
						</Fragment>
					</Card>

					<Card title="A short one" sub="cards are not all the same height">
						<p class="cm-card__text">
							The short card is the test. If it stretched to match
							the tall one, <code>align-items: start</code> and
							<code>height: 100%</code> are wrong.
						</p>
					</Card>
				</ul>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					<code>--accent</code> marks the one card a reader should look
					at first. It is a 2px rule, not a hue: the palette has none
					to spend, and a border cannot drift from the tokens in
					light or dark.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- ==================================================== prose
			     An article head, then the prose it introduces. The
			     <PostHead> import here is load-bearing: nothing else
			     in the showcase rendered it, so `astro build` never
			     compiled the component and a class it emits could
			     sit undefined in the stylesheet indefinitely. A
			     contract test now checks markup against selectors,
			     but demonstrating the component is what proves the
			     rule renders the way it reads. -->

			<section id="tiles">
				<SectionHead title="Tiles &amp; inline navigation" />
				<p class="lede">
					A tile is a bucket count with its bound under it. The tier is a
					<b>glyph</b>, not a tint, and it is a modifier that defaults to
					<i>unmarked</i> &mdash; a bucket with nothing in it has no mark at all,
					which is what keeps a healthy system from looking like a sick one.
				</p>

				<div class="cm-cards">
					<div class="cm-tile">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Healthy</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">4</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__note">&gt;30 days</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tile cm-tile--warn">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Expiring Soon</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">2</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__note">&lt;30 days</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tile cm-tile--err">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Expired</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">1</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-tile cm-tile--empty">
						<div class="cm-tile__label">Revoked</div>
						<div class="cm-tile__val">0</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="cm-kicker">
					All four are the same element. The only difference between "2
					expiring" and "0 revoked" is which class is on it &mdash; and the
					one with nothing in it is the quietest of the four.
				</p>

				<div class="cm-block">
					<p class="lede">
						Renew immediately on the
						<button class="cm-link" type="button">Certificates</button>
						page. As a <code>&#123;&lt;span onclick&#125;&#125;</code> that word has no role,
						no tab stop and never appears in a screen reader's list of
						controls; as a button it is one.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="prose" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="prose">
<code>.cm-prose</code> for the body of an article and <code>.cm-post-head</code> for the block above it. The post head is the one page head that carries a rule under itself, because the thing below it is prose and not another section.
				</SectionHead>

				<article>
					<PostHead
						title="Extract components from a design"
						date={new Date('2025-09-26')}
						updatedDate={new Date('2025-09-27')}
					/>
					<div class="cm-prose">
						<h2>A heading</h2>
						<p>oem-ui is the design language: sharp, flat, mono, high-contrast, dark by default. Transitions are <code>0.12s</code>, corners stay square, and borders do the lifting.</p>
						<blockquote>Only touch a line if you can explain why it moved. The layout must be stable between scrolling and non-scrolling pages.</blockquote>
						<pre><code>{`:root { --maxw: 860px; --gutter: 1.25rem; }`}</code></pre>
						</div>

						<!-- The specimen is a .cm-lede, NOT a .cm-prose paragraph,
						and that is the whole point of it. .cm-prose is itself
						--ink, so a <strong> inside it is invisible and proves
						nothing. The case this default exists for is emphasis
						inside DIM prose - a lede, a card body, an about block -
						which is where every consumer was re-declaring it. -->
						<p class="cm-lede">
						Emphasis is an element default, not a class, so this
						<strong>bold word</strong> needs nothing on it. A bare
						<code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> is how every consumer writes it, and
						the browser default carries no colour step at all: inside dim
						prose there is nothing but stroke weight to read. The library
						sets <code>--ink</code> and weight 700 together, so it
						survives greyscale and clears 16:1 on the surface behind it.
						</p>
											</article>

											<!-- ====================================================
											     prose table: a table INSIDE prose. The library had
											     `.cm-table`, which is a data grid - nowrap cells,
											     a sticky header, a capped scrollport. It is the
											     wrong component for an article, and the consumer
											     that needed one (hermes-articles, 397 markdown
											     pipe-tables across 34 articles) hand-rolled
											     `.article-content .table-wrapper` with
											     `min-width: max-content`, which puts a
											     horizontal scrollbar under EVERY table in the
											     article whether or not it needed one.

											     The fixture below is deliberately SIX columns of
											     sentences - the case that forces the decision.
											     Three columns would fit and prove nothing. -->
										<section id="prosetable" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
											<SectionHead title="prose table">
						<code>.cm-prose-table</code> is for a table that is part of a sentence-paragraph - a comparison, a set of facts. <code>.cm-table</code> is for a grid you scan across. The cells WRAP here instead of holding <code>nowrap</code>, so a six-column table in a 68ch measure degrades into readable rows rather than a scrollbar the reader has to discover mid-article, and there is no <code>max-height</code>, because the article page owns the vertical scroll and a cap would put a second scrollbar inside a paragraph. The last row shows the one thing that cannot wrap: a long unbreakable URL, which breaks rather than dragging the page wide with it.
											</SectionHead>

											<div class="cm-prose">
												<p>A comparison belongs in the flow of the prose, and this table has a
													caption and four rows of sentences:</p>
												<table class="cm-prose-table">
													<caption>Benchmarks measured on the same host, 2026-09-30.</caption>
													<thead>
														<tr>
															<th scope="col">Engine</th>
															<th scope="col">What it actually does</th>
															<th scope="col">Cold start</th>
															<th scope="col">Feasibility for this workload</th>
															<th scope="col">Falls over on</th>
															<th scope="col">Recommendation</th>
														</tr>
													</thead>
													<tbody>
														<tr>
															<td data-label="Engine">Signal generation</td>
															<td data-label="What it actually does">Analyzes price, volume and social data to output a buy, sell or hold signal.</td>
															<td data-label="Cold start">~400ms</td>
															<td data-label="Feasibility for this workload">Hard but achievable, and the only category where a human stays in the loop.</td>
															<td data-label="Falls over on">Regime change with no retrain.</td>
															<td data-label="Recommendation">Ship this first.</td>
														</tr>
														<tr>
															<td data-label="Engine">Strategy optimization</td>
															<td data-label="What it actually does">Backtests thousands of parameter combinations to find a profitable configuration.</td>
															<td data-label="Cold start">minutes</td>
															<td data-label="Feasibility for this workload">Moderate, and the output is only as honest as the historical data.</td>
															<td data-label="Falls over on">Look-ahead bias in the backtest.</td>
															<td data-label="Recommendation">Build after the first ships.</td>
														</tr>
														<tr>
															<td data-label="Engine">Fully autonomous</td>
															<td data-label="What it actually does">The AI decides what to trade, at what size, and executes with no human input.</td>
															<td data-label="Cold start">n/a</td>
															<td data-label="Feasibility for this workload">Extremely hard, and mostly fails in practice for retail-sized accounts.</td>
															<td data-label="Falls over on">Everything, at once, unattended.</td>
															<td data-label="Recommendation">Do not build this.</td>
														</tr>
														<tr>
															<td data-label="Engine">Audit trail</td>
															<td data-label="What it actually does">Records every decision and the data behind it, for a later reader to check.</td>
															<td data-label="Cold start">~0ms</td>
															<td data-label="Feasibility for this workload">Trivial, and the one thing worth building regardless of the other three.</td>
															<td data-label="Falls over on">Nothing, which is the point.</td>
															<td data-label="Recommendation">Long URL that cannot wrap: https://oem.ngo/docs/benchmark/2026/09/30/full-methodology-and-reproduction-notes-v2</td>
														</tr>
													</tbody>
												</table>
											</div>
										</section>
									</section>
			<!-- ==================================================== layout
			     The two page shapes that are not a single column: a lede
			     under the head, and a main block with a metadata aside.
			     Both were hand-rolled per consumer with a raw `ch` in
			     each file, so a change to reading measure never reached
			     any of them. The specimens below are drawn FROM the
			     tokens they document. -->
			<section id="layout" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="layout">
<code>.cm-lede</code> is the opening sentence under a page head, and <code>.cm-split</code> puts a metadata column beside a content block. The measure comes from <code>--measure</code>, not a number typed next to it, so changing the reading width is a token change. The split stacks below 700px on purpose: two columns of text on a phone is two unreadable columns.
				</SectionHead>

					<!-- `.cm-shell--rail` centres a PROSE measure in the space
					     beside the rail, which is right for a content page. An
					     application shell wants the whole column instead: with
					     the prose measure the page slid right as the window
					     widened (measured x=336 at 1280px, x=416 at 1440px),
					     because `margin-inline: auto` re-centres the capped
					     child in whatever room is left. `.cm-shell--rail--fill`
					     releases the cap AND the fixed `width: var(--maxw)`, so
					     the column is pinned to the rail's padding edge and
					     uses all the rest. Rendered nested here because the
					     showcase's own shell is the prose variant. -->
					<div class="cm-shell cm-shell--rail cm-shell--rail--fill"
						 style="border:1px solid var(--line);margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">
						<div style="padding:var(--space-4)">
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">
								An application column. It fills the space beside the rail
								instead of being centred in it, so the left edge stays
								put at every window width.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>

				<div class="cm-spec" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample"><span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0">The widest a reading column gets, in characters.</span></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>68ch</code></span>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure-narrow</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample"><span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0;max-width:var(--measure-narrow)">A compact block, not re-wrapped.</span></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>34ch</code></span>
					</div>
					<!-- The inline row's title column. It exists so a list
					     reads as a table: every excerpt starts at the same
					     offset instead of wherever the previous title
					     happened to end. Below 845px, and in the
					     1000-1055px rail band, the excerpt is handed off
					     rather than squeezed to a sliver — so this
					     specimen carries a live readout of whether the
					     column is currently in play, which is the whole
					     question a reader of this token should ask. -->
					<div class="cm-spec__row" data-spec="measure-title">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure-title</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample">
							<span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0;max-width:var(--measure-title)">The title column of a list row.</span>
						</span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>42ch</code></span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain">lede</h3>
				<p class="cm-lede">
					Read this before the rest. It is wider and dimmer than the
					body text, so the eye takes the sentence in once and then
					moves down to the content instead of reading the page twice.
				</p>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">key-value, wrapped pairs</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					HTML lets you group <code>dt</code>/<code>dd</code> inside a
					<code>div</code> when a row needs a control of its own - a link
					around the whole row, a toggle, a popover trigger. The
					wrapper is a real element, and a real element inside a
					<code>display:grid</code> parent becomes a grid
					<em>item</em>, which is how a two-column list silently turns
					into a grid of stacked blocks. The library takes the
					wrapper out of the layout with <code>display:contents</code>.
					Both lists below are the same markup; the second one only
					adds the wrapper. <strong>Check them at a desktop width</strong>
					- on a phone both stack to one column and look identical, so
					a narrow-viewport pass cannot see this at all.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-split">
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain">direct</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd>
							<dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd>
							<dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd>
						</dl>
					</div>
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain">wrapped</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<div><dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd></div>
							<div><dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd></div>
							<div><dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd></div>
						</dl>
					</div>
				</div>
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain">flush</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv cm-kv--flush">
							<div><dt>rows</dt><dd>4</dd></div>
							<div><dt>stack</dt><dd>v1.12</dd></div>
						</dl>
						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:var(--space-2) 0 0">
						<code>.cm-kv--flush</code> drops the block's own bottom margin:
						inside a dialog or a card the surrounding panel already
						provides that space, and cancelling it with an inline
						<code>style</code> is something a stylesheet cannot override
						or audit.
						</p>
					</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">key-value, whole row is the link</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					<code>.cm-kv--link</code> is the case above where the
					per-row control is an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>. Link only
					the <code>dt</code> and the target is the term - measured
					14px tall in WebKit at an iPhone viewport, a third of the
					44px floor, because <code>dt</code> is inline and
					<code>min-height</code> is ignored on an inline box. So
					the link wraps the row and owns the two columns, and the
					grid moves down one level onto it. <strong>Tap the
					gap between the term and the value</strong> and the
					row goes, not the term alone.
				</p>
				<dl class="cm-kv cm-kv--link">
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>role</dt>
							<dd>engineer</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>since</dt>
							<dd>2014</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>based</dt>
							<dd>remote</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
				</dl>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--plain" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">split</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					The main block and the aside below are the same DOM at every
					width; only the column count changes. Narrow the window past
					700px and the aside drops under the content rather than
					squeezing beside it.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-split">
					<div>
						<p style="margin-top:0">
							The main column keeps the reading measure and takes the
							wider share of the two. Nothing about it changes shape
							across the breakpoint, so the paragraph you read on a
							phone is the paragraph you read on a desktop.
						</p>
						<p>
							A second paragraph, so the column is taller than a single
							block of text and the gap between the columns is doing
							visible work.
						</p>
					</div>
					<aside class="cm-split__aside">
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd>
							<dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd>
							<dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd>
						</dl>
						<a class="cm-back" href="#layout">
							<span class="cm-back__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8592;</span>
							<span>back to layout</span>
						</a>
					</aside>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="forms" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:1.8rem">
				<SectionHead title="forms">
inputs are element defaults, so a bare <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> is already on-brand. <code>.cm-field</code> and friends are the layout around them.
				</SectionHead>
				<form class="cm-form" onsubmit="return false">
					<div class="cm-field-row">
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-name">handle <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
							<input id="f-name" type="text" placeholder="omiinaya" required />
							<span class="cm-field__help">lowercase, no spaces.</span>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-kind">kind</label>
							<select id="f-kind">
								<option>site</option>
								<option>repo</option>
								<option>service</option>
							</select>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-url">url <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
						<input id="f-url" type="url" placeholder="https://" aria-invalid="true" />
						<span class="cm-field__error">must include the scheme.</span>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-notes">notes</label>
						<textarea id="f-notes" rows="3" placeholder="what is it for?"></textarea>
					</div>

					<fieldset class="cm-field">
						<legend>visibility</legend>
						<label><input type="checkbox" checked /> listed in nav</label>
						<label><input type="checkbox" /> pinned</label>
					</fieldset>

					<fieldset class="cm-field">
						<legend>access</legend>
						<label><input type="radio" name="f-access" checked /> public</label>
						<label><input type="radio" name="f-access" /> unlisted</label>
					</fieldset>

					<!-- a control that owns an affordance. The toggle lives INSIDE
						     the field, on its right edge, so it acts on the value it reveals
						     and keeps a full tap square. It is deliberately NOT inside
						     .cm-field-row - that is the two-FIELDS grid, and a button in it
						     wraps to a second row and stops lining up with the input. -->
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-pw">password</label>
							<div class="cm-field__control">
								<input id="f-pw" type="password" placeholder="&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;" />
								<button type="button" class="cm-icon-btn cm-icon-btn--bare" aria-label="show password">
									<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
										<path d="M1 12s4-8 11-8 11 8 11 8-4 8-11 8-11-8-11-8z" />
										<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" />
									</svg>
								</button>
							</div>
							<span class="cm-field__help">the toggle is <code>--tap</code> square and overlaps the field padding, so the text never runs under it.</span>
						</div>

					<div class="cm-form__actions">
						<button type="reset" class="cm-btn">cancel</button>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary">save</button>
					</div>
				</form>
			</section>

			<!-- ====================================================== auth
			     Sign-in is not its own design language. It is the card,
			     the field, the alert and the button this page already
			     demonstrates, arranged for credentials - so the only NEW
			     surface is the centred frame and the code field, and
			     everything else is composition. The frame is shown
			     scaled into a frame rather than at 100dvh, because a
			     100dvh specimen inside a scrolling page pushes every
			     other section a full screen down. -->
			<section id="auth" class="cm-section cm-section--pad" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-7)">
				<SectionHead title="auth">
Every class below except <code>.cm-auth</code>, <code>.cm-card--auth</code>, <code>.cm-code-input</code> and <code>.cm-divider</code> was already in this system. A sign-in screen is the card, the field, the alert and the button you have just seen, arranged for credentials.
				</SectionHead>

				<div class="cm-spec">
					<div class="cm-auth" style="min-height:420px;background:var(--bg-2)">
						<div class="cm-card cm-card--auth">
							<div class="cm-card__head">
								<h3 class="cm-card__title">sign in</h3>
								<p class="cm-card__sub">use your oem account.</p>
							</div>
							<div class="cm-card__body">
								<form class="cm-form" onsubmit="return false">
									<div class="cm-field">
										<label class="cm-field__label" for="a-email">email</label>
										<input id="a-email" type="email" autocomplete="username" placeholder="you@mrx.sh" />
									</div>
									<div class="cm-field">
										<label class="cm-field__label" for="a-pass">password</label>
										<input id="a-pass" type="password" autocomplete="current-password" placeholder="••••••••" />
										<span class="cm-field__help">at least 12 characters.</span>
									</div>
									<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--err">
										<span class="cm-alert__mark"></span>
										<div class="cm-alert__body">
											<p class="cm-alert__text">that password was not recognised.</p>
										</div>
									</div>
									<div class="cm-field">
										<label class="cm-field__label" for="a-code">authenticator code</label>
										<input id="a-code" class="cm-code-input" type="text" inputmode="numeric"
											maxlength="6" autocomplete="one-time-code" placeholder="000000" />
										<span class="cm-field__help">6 digits, from your authenticator app.</span>
									</div>
									<div class="cm-divider">or continue with</div>
									<button type="button" class="cm-btn cm-btn--block">sign in with passkey</button>
									<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary cm-btn--block">sign in</button>
								</form>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="cm-head__sub" style="padding:var(--space-3) var(--space-4)">
						<code>.cm-auth</code> is the centred page frame and owns the
						measure (<code>max-width</code> on its child, not on itself,
						so the card can never be wider than the padding allows).
						<code>.cm-code-input</code> is the only genuinely new
						control: a code must not read as prose, so it is centred
						and tracked out. <code>.cm-divider</code> is a flex row
						with two growing rules - a <code>border-top</code> on the
						label leaves a gap no padding closes.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h3 style="margin-top:var(--space-5)">a wider frame</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-3)">
					<code>.cm-auth--wide</code> is the same frame at a larger
					measure, for a page that has to fit more than a
					credential pair - a register form, or a sign-in with
					side-by-side providers. It is <em>wider</em>, not
					<em>looser</em>: the field gap and the label size are
					unchanged, so it is the same screen at a different
					amount of it.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-spec">
					<div class="cm-auth cm-auth--wide" style="min-height:280px;background:var(--bg-2)">
						<div class="cm-card cm-card--auth">
